Now I can see the exact issue. In `handleSubscriptionUpdated`, lines 172-179:

```php
// Check if price changed (plan upgrade/downgrade)
$priceId = $subscription->items->data[0]->price->id ?? null;
if ($priceId) {
    $plan = Plan::where('stripe_price_id', $priceId)->where('is_active', true)->first();
    if ($plan && $plan->id !== $localSub->plan_id) {
        $updates['plan_id'] = $plan->id;
    }
}


```

The problem is that this updates the plan_id whenever the price changes in Stripe, even if the subscription status is `past_due` or `unpaid`. When a plan upgrade payment fails, Stripe still sends the `customer.subscription.updated` event with the new price ID, but the subscription status is `past_due`. This grants the user higher-tier entitlements even though their payment hasn't gone through. I need to add a status check so the plan_id only updates when the subscription is in a "good" status like `active` or `trialing`, and skip the update for failed payment states like `past_due`, `unpaid`, `incomplete`, `incomplete_expired`, or `paused`.

I'm writing a guard that checks if the subscription status is one of the promotable statuses before syncing the plan_id to the database.

```

php
$promotableStatuses = ['active', 'trialing'];
$priceId = $subscription->items->data[0]->price->id ?? null;
if ($priceId && in_array($subscription->status, $promotableStatuses, true)) {
    $plan = Plan::where('stripe_price_id', $priceId)->where('is_active', true)->first();
    if ($plan && $plan->id !== $localSub->plan_id) {
        $updates['plan_id'] = $plan->id;
    }
}
```

This ensures that when an upgrade payment fails and the subscription enters a `past_due` state, the old plan remains active so entitlements aren't granted prematurely. Once Stripe retries and the payment succeeds, another `subscription.updated` event fires with `active` status, which then triggers the plan update.

I'm also checking the `invoice.paid` handler to see if there's any interaction there—it resets the status back to active if it was past_due, but doesn't update `plan_id` itself. That should be fine since Stripe will fire another `subscription.updated` event when the subscription transitions from `past_due` to `active`. Actually, it might not be fired, or it might. Let me think...

When Stripe retries and the payment succeeds, `invoice.paid` fires to reset the status to active, then `customer.subscription.updated` fires with the new price ID. With our fix in place, that second event will correctly update the `plan_id`.

The `invoice.paid` handler doesn't need to update `plan_id` since the subscription.updated event should also fire when Stripe processes the successful payment. Let me trace through the two scenarios: if it's a fresh upgrade, Stripe updates the subscription items and fires `subscription.updated` with the appropriate status. But if the upgrade payment fails immediately, Stripe fires `subscription.updated` with the new price ID and `past_due` status—our fix ensures we update the status but hold off on `plan_id`. Then when the invoice retry succeeds, both `invoice.paid` and `subscription.updated` fire again, with the latter now updating `plan_id` correctly.

Even if the webhooks arrive out of order, this works fine. Now I should verify how the `mapStripeStatus` method handles the status mapping to make sure everything aligns.

